## Is heart failure a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Most people with heart failure are covered.

## Reasonable adjustments

- less physical work, or no heavy lifting
- flexible or reduced hours to manage tiredness
- time off for appointments
- working closer to the entrance, or on the ground floor
- working from home

## Benefits

PIP is based on how heart failure affects you on most days. Breathlessness and tiredness can affect activities such as:

- **moving around**, if you cannot walk more than a short distance reliably
- **washing and bathing**, and **dressing**, if you need to stop and rest
- **preparing food**, if you cannot stand for long

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you are over State Pension age, look at [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) instead of PIP. It is for people who need help with personal care or supervision.

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.

## If you are terminally ill

If a doctor or nurse thinks you may have 12 months or less to live, you can claim benefits under **special rules**, which are faster and give you the highest rates.

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
